(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology II Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which is the central education point for a patient with erythema nodosum?","choices":["It is a reaction pattern rather than an isolated disease, so identifying and treating the trigger is the point","It is an infection of the subcutaneous fat that requires a prolonged course of oral antibiotics","It is a chronic condition that will require lifelong immunosuppressive therapy","It is a premalignant change that requires excision of the affected nodules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is why the workup matters as much as the symptomatic care. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which referral is named for palmar hyperhidrosis refractory to all other treatments?","choices":["Endocrinology, for assessment of thyroid and adrenal function","Psychiatry, since refractory palmar sweating is usually anxiety-driven","Thoracic surgery, for endoscopic thoracic sympathectomy","Dermatology, for a further trial of topical aluminium chloride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — dermatology handles botulinum toxin, microwave thermolysis and glycopyrronium. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What must be checked before starting dapsone for dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Serum immunoglobulin A level to exclude selective deficiency","HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 typing to confirm susceptibility","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status","Thyroid-stimulating hormone and anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the blood count is then monitored for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does biopsy show in granuloma annulare?","choices":["Palisading granulomas with central necrobiosis and mucin deposition","Septal panniculitis without vasculitis and Miescher's granulomas","A dense neutrophilic infiltrate at the ulcer edge","Interface dermatitis with scattered necrotic keratinocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — thought to involve a type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction around degenerating collagen. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the gold standard test for determining the cleavage plane in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Serum enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay for structural protein antibodies","Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Skin biopsy with direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin G","Genetic testing of the known structural protein genes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with immunofluorescence antigen mapping used to localise missing or reduced proteins. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which organism contributes to papulopustular rosacea?","choices":["Demodex folliculorum, whose overgrowth drives inflammation","Malassezia, whose overgrowth drives inflammation in sebum-rich areas","Staphylococcus aureus, colonising the follicles","Cutibacterium acnes, proliferating within blocked follicles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why ivermectin 1% cream outperforms metronidazole in Demodex-associated disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for erythema multiforme?","choices":["Recurrent herpes simplex infection, immunosuppression, high-risk medications, recent respiratory infection and a prior episode","Chronic venous insufficiency, obesity, immobility, previous deep vein thrombosis and chronic lower-limb oedema","Cumulative ultraviolet exposure, fair skin, outdoor work, immunosuppression and older age","Nasal staphylococcal carriage, obesity, diabetes, occlusive clothing and humid weather"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a prior episode itself carries recurrence risk. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is the diagnosis of pyoderma gangrenosum established?","choices":["By wound culture growing a characteristic organism","By serum antibody testing against dermal structural proteins","By exclusion, since there is no gold standard test","By biopsy showing a pathognomonic palisading granuloma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — biopsy from the ulcer edge shows dense neutrophils and rules out malignancy and infection. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What distinguishes phototoxicity from photoallergy?","choices":["Phototoxicity is non-immunologic and dose-dependent and occurs on first exposure; photoallergy is immunologic and dose-independent and requires prior sensitisation","Phototoxicity is immunologic and requires prior sensitisation before any reaction; photoallergy is non-immunologic, dose-dependent and occurs on the very first exposure","Phototoxicity affects only sun-exposed skin; photoallergy affects only covered skin","Phototoxicity resolves on stopping the drug; photoallergy always resolves immediately too"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — phototoxicity resembles an exaggerated sunburn within hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What distinguishes solar lentigo from lentigo maligna?","choices":["Solar lentigo has uniform pigmentation; lentigo maligna is asymmetric with irregular border and colour","Solar lentigo is raised and rough; lentigo maligna is flat and smooth","Solar lentigo appears in early childhood; lentigo maligna appears only after the age of seventy","Solar lentigo appears on covered skin; lentigo maligna on exposed skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and dermoscopy is essential — solar lentigo shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What is first-line systemic therapy for acute rapidly progressive pyoderma gangrenosum?","choices":["Dapsone at 100 mg daily by mouth","Doxycycline at 100 mg twice daily","Prednisone at 0.5 to 1 mg/kg daily","Infliximab at 5 mg/kg intravenously"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ciclosporin at 3 to 5 mg/kg daily is the alternative. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which referral is named when squamous or basal cell carcinoma is confirmed in a photoaged patient?","choices":["Plastic surgery, for advanced cosmetic restoration","Genetics, for suspected DNA repair disorder assessment","Rheumatology, for the connective tissue changes of solar elastosis","Dermatologic surgery, including Mohs surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — oncology handles melanoma and advanced squamous cell carcinoma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"What drives the commonest, insulin-resistant form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation","Autoimmune destruction of the melanocytes within the affected intertriginous skin","Chronic ultraviolet exposure driving proliferation of the basal layer melanocytes and increased melanin production","Immunoglobulin A deposition in the dermal papillae activating neutrophils"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Malignant, drug-induced and endocrine forms make up the rest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the underlying mechanism of dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Immunoglobulin E mediated mast cell degranulation with histamine release","Immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase, depositing in the dermal papillae","Immunoglobulin G antibodies directed against the epithelial basement membrane zone of the skin","Immunoglobulin G antibodies against keratinocyte adhesion molecules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, in genetically susceptible individuals consuming gluten, producing subepidermal blisters. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How much do tanning beds reduce risk compared with natural sun, and how are they classified?","choices":["They halve ultraviolet B exposure while delivering the same ultraviolet A dose","They are safer for Fitzpatrick types I and II under supervision","They are acceptable if limited to the months outside peak summer","They are not safer at all, and are classified as Group 1 carcinogens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — there is no safe level of tanning. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What is the immunological basis of dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G antibodies against desmoglein between the keratinocytes","A T-cell mediated destruction of melanocytes within the affected epidermis","Immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase, forming complexes that deposit in the dermal papillae","Immunoglobulin G antibodies directed against the epidermal basement membrane zone at the dermal junction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — complement and neutrophils are then activated, producing subepidermal blisters. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What mechanism underlies the most common form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Autoantibody deposition along the epithelial basement membrane zone","Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation","A paraneoplastic process driven by an underlying gastric adenocarcinoma secreting growth factors that act on the epidermis","A delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction to a contacted allergen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the insulin-resistant or benign form is the commonest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does direct immunofluorescence show in erythema multiforme, and why does it matter?","choices":["It shows intercellular immunoglobulin G throughout the epidermis, confirming the diagnosis","It is negative, which distinguishes erythema multiforme from pemphigoid and pemphigus","It shows granular immunoglobulin A in the dermal papillae, confirming the diagnosis","It shows linear immunoglobulin G at the basement membrane zone, confirming the diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy shows interface dermatitis with vacuolar degeneration of the basal layer and necrotic keratinocytes. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What triggers the majority of erythema multiforme cases?","choices":["Sulfonamide antibiotics, accounting for over 50% of cases","Mycoplasma pneumoniae, accounting for over 50% of cases","Idiopathic causes, accounting for over 50% of cases","Herpes simplex virus, accounting for over 50% of cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Other infectious causes include Mycoplasma pneumoniae, histoplasmosis, Epstein-Barr virus and coxsackievirus. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which rosacea subtype is most common, and what defines it?","choices":["Ocular, with blepharitis, conjunctivitis and meibomian gland dysfunction","Erythematotelangiectatic, with persistent central facial erythema, flushing and telangiectasias but no papules or pustules","Papulopustular, with transient central facial papules and pustules on a background of erythema and no comedones","Phymatous, with skin thickening and nodular enlargement of the nose and chin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the skin is often described as sensitive and stinging. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which type of epidermolysis bullosa is most common, and what is its cleavage level?","choices":["Junctional epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with cleavage in the lamina lucida","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with sub-lamina densa cleavage","Kindler epidermolysis bullosa, at about 70% of cases, with mixed cleavage planes","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex, at about 70% of cases, with intraepidermal cleavage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it results from keratin 5 and 14 mutations and is autosomal dominant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How is an isolated actinic keratosis usually treated?","choices":["Imiquimod applied twice weekly for sixteen weeks","Photodynamic therapy using aminolevulinic acid and light","Cryotherapy with two to three freeze-thaw cycles","5-fluorouracil cream applied for two to four weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the standard of care for isolated lesions, with shave excision or curettage as alternatives. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Which biopsy finding is described as pathognomonic across the Stevens-Johnson and toxic epidermal necrolysis spectrum?","choices":["Septal panniculitis without any accompanying vasculitis","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with dermal-epidermal junction separation","Granular immunoglobulin A deposited within the dermal papillae of the skin","Palisading granulomas surrounding an area of necrobiosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, on punch biopsy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which feature separates photodermatitis from seborrhoeic dermatitis on the face?","choices":["Photodermatitis produces greasy yellow scale, which seborrhoeic dermatitis lacks","Photodermatitis is confined to the scalp, which seborrhoeic dermatitis spares","Photodermatitis spares the nasolabial folds, which seborrhoeic dermatitis involves","Photodermatitis involves the nasolabial folds, which seborrhoeic dermatitis spares"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — shadowed areas are protected from light. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What does skin biopsy show in polymorphous light eruption, and how useful is it?","choices":["Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with dermal-epidermal separation — pathognomonic","Granular immunoglobulin A at the dermal papillae — diagnostic in every case","Solar elastosis with tangled elastin fibres — the defining histological change","A perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate with dermal oedema — supportive but not pathognomonic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is largely clinical, from history and morphology. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"How are acute and chronic urticaria distinguished?","choices":["By severity — chronic urticaria involves more of the body surface","By trigger — chronic urticaria always has an identifiable cause","By age — chronic urticaria occurs only in adults over forty","By duration — chronic urticaria has persisted beyond six weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both are mast cell-mediated, with transient pruritic wheals. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the time course of sunburn after exposure?","choices":["Onset within minutes of exposure, peaking within the first hour","Onset thirty minutes to hours after exposure, resolving in seven to ten days","Onset two to three days after exposure, peaking at one week","Onset three to five hours after exposure, peaking at twelve to twenty-four hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why the injury is often underestimated at the time. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which test is mandatory when investigating polymorphous light eruption?","choices":["Skin biopsy, which shows a pathognomonic pattern","An antinuclear antibody panel, to exclude lupus erythematosus","A porphyrin screen of urine, stool and red cells in every patient","Photopatch testing with the standard photoallergen series"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, especially anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What is the most common infectious trigger of erythema nodosum?","choices":["Group A Streptococcus","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Herpes simplex virus","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside Yersinia, tuberculosis, coccidioidomycosis, histoplasmosis and hepatitis B and C. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How does polymorphous light eruption typically present?","choices":["Persistent eczematous change in chronically exposed skin of older males","Papules appearing thirty minutes to hours after exposure on the décolletage, forearms and dorsal hands, sparing chronically exposed skin","Painful erythema developing within minutes of ultraviolet exposure and resolving within an hour of moving into the shade","An exaggerated sunburn on first exposure to a new medication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, resolving in seven to ten days without scarring and recurring each spring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 105"}]};
